"Bluefin Tuna","type":"production title"},{"iso_3166_1":"JP","title":"プライムタイム","type":""},{"iso_3166_1":"LT","title":"Didysis eteris","type":""},{"iso_3166_1":"CA","title":"En direct","type":"Quebec"},{"iso_3166_1":"FR","title":"En direct","type":""},{"iso_3166_1":"KR","title":"프라임타임","type":""},{"iso_3166_1":"RU","title":"Прайм-тайм","type":""},{"iso_3166_1":"UA","title":"Праймтайм","type":""}]},"reviews":{"page":1,"results":[{"author":"TheFilmCritics.org","author_details":{"name":"TheFilmCritics.org","username":"thefilmcritics","avatar_path":"/dWhq1YiuNZrc299ejGy82xhmnJU.png","rating":4},"content":"A true-crime story this inherently bizarre should not require much embellishment, yet *Primetime* spends so much energy dressing itself up that the actual story gets buried underneath the filmmaking. What could have been a sharp, uncomfortable examination of television, celebrity, and the ethics of true crime instead becomes a frustratingly uneven film that mistakes stylistic excess for substance.\r\n\r\nRobert Pattinson gives a pretty good performance as Chris Hansen, bringing enough conviction to make the central figure compelling even when the screenplay does not. The supporting cast is strong as well. Skyler Gisondo makes an impression as the adult decoy pretending to be an underage boy, while Merritt Wever brings welcome dimension as the show’s producer. There are also genuinely interesting ideas scattered throughout the film, particularly in its examination of how media can commoditize real human suffering into entertainment.\r\n\r\nThe problem is that *Primetime* rarely develops its ideas into a cohesive story. It often feels less like a narrative and more like a collection of scenes placed next to one another. The result makes it difficult for viewers to become invested in what is happening or, more importantly, in the characters involved.\r\n\r\nIts aesthetic ambitions are particularly exhausting. The fuzzy mid-2000s look is clearly deliberate, and there is nothing wrong with leaning into the visual language of the period. Here, though, the music, lighting, camera angles, and visual flourishes are pushed so aggressively that the movie starts announcing its artistry rather than","created_at":"2026-09-26T20:53:05Z","id":"6ab830b14d49a538e1e43c1e","updated_at":"2026-09-26T20:53:05Z","url":"https://www.themoviedb.org/review/6ab830b14d49a538e1e43c1e"},{"author":"Brent Marchant","author_details":{"name":"Brent Marchant","username":"Brent_Marchant","avatar_path":null,"rating":8},"content":"It’s been said that we often become what we concentrate upon. That’s ironic, because frequently this results in the creation of that which we’re purposely trying to avoid. And, in this third feature outing from director Lance Oppenheim, that’s precisely what happens to its high-profile protagonist, investigative journalist Chris Hansen (Robert Pattinson). In this fact-based satire, the film follows the ascendancy of the reporter on the tabloid TV series *Dateline NBC*, specifically through his work on the show’s *To Catch a Predator* segment (2004-2007) in which he covered stories designed to expose the exploits of pedophiles. Working in conjunction with the website Perverted-Justice, Hansen, his producer (Merritt Wever), and a pair of predator bait decoy actors (Skyler Gisondo, Phoebe Bridgers) orchestrated sting operations aimed at capturing their unsuspecting prey in the act on camera. While it’s undeniable that the intentions with this mission were fundamentally noble, their on-air depictions increasingly took on a vibe of sensationalism, blurring the line between journalism and entertainment. And, as Hansen’s star rose, he relished the personal notoriety and ratings popularity that came with his efforts. However, as the film postulates, his increasingly aggressive reporting tactics and quest for professional glory suggest that he was becoming as much of a predator on the alleged perpetrators as they were on their would-be victims. It might even be said that the methods employed verged on a form of entrapment, an approach that carried significantly murky and curiously i","created_at":"2026-10-01T06:44:08Z","id":"6abe013863858877fe8a1d46","updated_at":"2026-10-01T06:44:08Z","url":"https://www.themoviedb.org/review/6abe013863858877fe8a1d46"},{"author":"GenerationofSwine","author_details":{"name":"","username":"GenerationofSwine","avatar_path":"/xYhvrFNntgAowjRsf6mRg9JgITr.jpg","rating":1},"content":"What I learned from Primetime is that catching child predators is \"right wing\" and \"egotistical\" and \"bad.\" Which about sums up why all the heavily leftwing political injections makes movies suck. Sort of lost the plot when you spend an entire movie making the point that the dude who was CATCHING CHILD PREDATORS was WORSE than the actual child predators.  Seriously, Hollywood, you lost the plot and we don't think you're ever going to find it again.","created_at":"2026-10-04T21:11:29Z","id":"6ac2c100e24d7a0034454f6a","updated_at":"2026-10-04T21:11:29Z","url":"https://www.themoviedb.org/review/6ac2c100e24d7a0034454f6a"}],"total_pages":1,"total_results":3}}
